  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    At least one factor is fixed. The long run is the period in which every factor can change — it is defined by flexibility, not by a number of months.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Capital, in the form of plant and machinery. Labour is the variable factor in almost every textbook table.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Total output. The first option is average product and the third is marginal product.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    TP ÷ quantity of the variable factor. The last option is average revenue, a different ratio altogether.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The change in the total. Marginal always means *the change in*, and here it is the change in output, not in cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    30 − 20 = 10. The 12 is the second worker's marginal product, and 30 is the total product of three workers.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    36 ÷ 4 = 9. The 6 is the fourth worker's marginal product, which is a different figure.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Marginal product eventually falls. Total output can keep rising for a long time afterwards, which is why the first option is wrong.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ratio changes: one machine shared among more workers means each additional worker adds less. The law describes a ratio, not a worker's character.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    MP positive but below AP, with AP falling and TP still rising. Stage three is negative MP and falling TP, which no firm chooses.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zero. A positive marginal product adds to the total and a negative one subtracts, so the peak is exactly where MP passes through zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    AP's maximum. While MP is above AP the average rises, and while it is below the average falls, so the crossing is the turning point.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Constant, and paid even at zero output. Variable cost is the one that is zero when output is zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    TFC + TVC. The last option is a rearrangement of the profit identity, not a definition of cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    300 − 220 = ₦80. The ₦60 is the marginal cost of the second unit, and ₦100 is average variable cost at two units.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    300 ÷ 3 = ₦100. Average cost always divides total cost by output, not variable cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It falls continuously, because a fixed sum is divided by a growing output. It is the only one of the cost curves that never turns upward.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    TVC ÷ Q. The second is average total cost and the fourth is average fixed cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    AC = AVC + AFC, so AC is always above AVC by the amount of AFC, and the gap narrows as output rises.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    At the minimum. While MC is below AC the average falls, and while it is above the average rises, so the crossing is the minimum.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Falling: an addition below the average pulls the average down. The reverse is true when MC is above AC.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    P × Q. Total cost plus profit is an identity for revenue, not a definition, and it cannot be calculated without the profit.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    With the price fixed, TR = P × Q, so revenue rises in a straight line from the origin. A falling revenue curve belongs to a firm that must cut its price to sell more.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Below price, because selling one more unit requires cutting the price on all units, so revenue rises by less than the price of the extra unit.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    MR = MC. AR = AC is the break-even condition and maximising revenue ignores cost altogether.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Profit per unit is ₦15 and 15 × 200 = ₦3,000. The ₦12,000 is total revenue, and ₦9,000 is total cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    100Q = 400 + 50Q, so 50Q = 400 and Q = 8. The 4 comes from dividing the fixed cost by the price alone.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Revenue equals cost, so profit is zero — the firm earns only normal profit, which is the minimum needed to stay in business.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Minimum AVC. Below it the firm cannot cover its variable cost, so every unit produced adds to the loss.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Continue: the firm covers its variable cost and part of its fixed cost, so it loses less by producing than by stopping.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    All factors can vary. The long run is the planning horizon in which the scale of the firm itself can be chosen.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Falling long-run average cost as scale increases — bulk buying, specialisation, better machinery, cheaper finance.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rising long-run average cost: management becomes unwieldy, coordination costs rise, and the firm becomes too large to respond quickly.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The minimum of the long-run average cost curve, where economies of scale are exhausted and diseconomies have not yet begun.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    120 + 280 = ₦400. The ₦160 is the fixed cost alone and ₦280 the variable cost alone.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    400 ÷ 4 = ₦100. The ₦70 is average variable cost (280 ÷ 4) and ₦30 is average fixed cost (120 ÷ 4).
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is the cost of one more unit, which is exactly what a firm compares with the revenue from that unit when choosing output.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    MC above minimum AVC: below that point the firm shuts down and supplies nothing, so only the upper part of the curve is its supply.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    TC − TFC = 520 − 120 = ₦400. Adding the two instead of subtracting gives the ₦640 that the last option offers.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Thirty units: at MR = MC with MC rising, every unit beyond 30 costs more than it earns and every unit short of 30 could still earn a profit.
  ]

]

